<!-- slide: date 3 September 2026 -->

Everything in this talk runs. The data is the real ElixirConf US 2025 programme, taken from the published schedule, and every number on these slides is calculated by the notebook.

* Tempo answers *when is this free?*
* Agenda answers *what should I book, and where?*
* Built on Tempo, which treats every date and time as an interval
* Today: the real ElixirConf US 2025 programme, laid out from scratch

## Locale

Every date and time on these slides is rendered by Localize from a *named width* — `:short`, `:medium` — rather than a format string, so the output follows the locale rather than a pattern someone typed. Change it here and the whole deck follows.

`en-AU` is not bundled with Localize, so this also lets it fetch the locale on demand. Run this cell once before presenting and it is cached; without the download the deck falls back to `en` and renders American dates.

<!-- slide: code -->

```elixir
Application.put_env(:localize, :allow_runtime_locale_download, true)

{:ok, locale} = Localize.put_locale("en-AU")
to_string(locale)
```

## What we keep rebuilding

Every scheduling system I have worked on rebuilds the same three things above the calendar, and none of them is the interesting part of the product.

* A map of resources, and hand-rolled filters over its keys
* A travel matrix, hard-coded, usually wrong
* Free/busy state stored somewhere, and slowly going stale

The third one is the killer. Once availability is *stored* rather than *derived*, every code path that books something has to remember to release it, and one that forgets leaves a room permanently missing.

## The five nouns

Five nouns, and the last three are a sequence: you describe a **session**, `plan/3` returns every **arrangement** that would satisfy it, and accepting one writes **allocations**. One want, many candidates, one booking.

* **Resource** — a named thing that can be allocated: a room, a person, a projector
* **Place** — a container of resources and other places, so travel time is *derived* rather than configured
* **Session** — *one thing you want held*: how long, inside what window, what it needs
* **Arrangement** — *one of the many ways that session could be held*: a time, plus a resource for each thing it needs
* **Allocation** — *what accepting one arrangement leaves behind*: a resource booked to a session

## How they constrain each other

The five nouns say what *exists*; these say what *limits what*. Each line holds two words that most schedulers merge into one, and every merge is a bug — so Agenda keeps them apart.

* A resource is **open** when it could be used, **busy** where something holds it, **free** where open remains — *Biscayne: open all morning, busy for the panel, free either side*.
* A resource's **concurrency** is how many sessions may hold it at one moment; its **limit** is how many across a stretch of calendar — *one talk at a time, but at most two a day*.
* A session's **needs** *describe* a resource for Agenda to choose — *any room seating 250*; its **roster** *names* one exactly — *Anna Sherman, nobody else*.
* A **session** is one thing to be held; a **track** is sessions sharing an audience, so no two may run at once — *one talk, versus everything one delegate plans to attend*.

## The conference

Two days at the Renaissance Orlando at SeaWorld, three parallel rooms, and a fourth room used for registration, coffee, lunch and the reception rather than for talks.

| What | ElixirConf US 2025 |
| --- | --- |
| Days | Thursday 28 – Friday 29 August 2025 |
| Talk rooms | Peninsula 4-7, Canaveral, Biscayne |
| Breakout talks | 42 |
| Plenaries | 4 keynotes and updates |
| Session lengths | 20 and 40 minutes |
| Speakers | 45 |

## Breaks are not constraints

The first thing to model is when the rooms are *open*, and it is not a range — talks run 10:45 to 12:45, break for lunch, resume at 13:45, break again for coffee.

Lunch is not a rule to enforce. It is simply time the rooms are not open, and every calculation downstream inherits that for free.

<!-- slide: code -->

```elixir
import Agenda.Predicate
import Tempo.Sigils

alias Agenda.Arrangement

talk_hours =
  Tempo.IntervalSet.new!([
    ~o"2025-08-28T10:45/T12:45",
    ~o"2025-08-28T13:45/T15:05",
    ~o"2025-08-28T15:35/T16:05",
    ~o"2025-08-29T10:40/T12:40",
    ~o"2025-08-29T13:40/T15:20",
    ~o"2025-08-29T15:50/T16:20"
  ])

Tempo.IntervalSet.count(talk_hours)
```

## The rooms

Peninsula 4-7 is also the plenary room, so it opens earlier and closes later than the other two.

<!-- slide: code -->

```elixir
venue = Agenda.place("Renaissance Orlando at SeaWorld")

plenary_hours =
  Tempo.IntervalSet.new!([
    ~o"2025-08-28T09:00/T10:15",
    ~o"2025-08-28T10:45/T12:45",
    ~o"2025-08-28T13:45/T15:05",
    ~o"2025-08-28T15:35/T17:50",
    ~o"2025-08-29T09:00/T10:10",
    ~o"2025-08-29T10:40/T12:40",
    ~o"2025-08-29T13:40/T15:20",
    ~o"2025-08-29T15:50/T17:30"
  ])

room = fn name, seats, hours ->
  {:ok, resource} = Agenda.resource(name, within: venue, seats: seats) |> Agenda.open(hours)
  resource
end

peninsula = room.("Peninsula 4-7", 700, plenary_hours)
canaveral = room.("Canaveral", 250, talk_hours)
biscayne = room.("Biscayne", 250, talk_hours)

rooms = [peninsula, canaveral, biscayne]
Enum.map(rooms, & &1.name)
```

## Speakers are resources

This is the one idea in the talk. Not an attribute of a talk, not a string in a metadata map — a resource, exactly like a room, with the same default concurrency of one.

* A room holds one session at a time. So does a person.
* Nothing else has to change: same struct, same availability, same clash rule
* Rooms are chosen by description; people are named
* This is what invented demo data never has

## Why it matters here

I did not plan this example around the constraint. I loaded the real programme and the constraint was already in it.

* Allison Randal keynoted Thursday 09:15 — and joined the 10:45 panel
* Anna Sherman was on that panel, and gave a talk of her own as well
* Four people shared the panel between them
* A scheduler treating a speaker as a label cannot see any of this

## Forty-five speakers

One resource each, open for the hours of the conference. Nothing about them is speaker-specific: `role: :speaker` is an ordinary attribute of the kind you would put on a room, and `by_name` is only a lookup so that a talk can name the people who must be in it.

<!-- slide: code -->

```elixir
speaker_names =
  ~w(Hugo|Baraúna Misael|Perez_Chamorro Savannah|Manning Anna|Sherman Allison|Randal
     Lorena|Mireles Chris|Nelson Aaron|Votre Carlos|Souza Emmanuel|Pinault Jordan|Miller
     Britton|Broderick Dennis|Beatty Nicholas|Henry Yuri|Oliveira Stephanie|Lane
     Joe|Harrow Juan|Azambuja Krzysztof|Nalepa Noah|Betzen Nathan|Hessler
     Mitchell|Hanberg Jacob|Swanner Adam|Kirk George|Guimarães Jinkyou|Son Matt|Ludwigs
     Tyler|Young Jonatan|Männchen John|Darrington Christian|Koch Jeremy|Searls
     Igor|Spiridonov Gonzalo|Rodriguez Michael|St_Clair Micah|Woods Ivy|Markwell
     Kate|Rezentes Paulo|Valente Daniel|Kentfield Dan|Janowski Brett|Beatty
     German|Velasco Doug|VonMoser Geoffrey|Lessel)
  |> Enum.map(&(&1 |> String.replace("|", " ") |> String.replace("_", " ")))

speakers =
  Enum.map(speaker_names, fn name ->
    {:ok, speaker} = Agenda.resource(name, role: :speaker) |> Agenda.open(plenary_hours)
    speaker
  end)

by_name = Map.new(speakers, &{&1.name, &1})
length(speakers)
```

## One roster call per role

A trap worth knowing, because it silently costs you the exact constraint this talk is about.

* `roster(:speaker, [a, b, c])` binds all three
* `roster(:speaker, [a])` three times keeps only the last
* No error, no warning — the panel simply loses three of its four people

## The programme

Forty-two talks, each naming its duration, the kind of room it needs and the people who must be there — and nothing about when. Agenda has the two days from the programme and the open hours from the rooms; which talk lands on which day is its problem, not the data's.

That makes the layout **repeatable but not predetermined**. The same input gives the same answer every time, but nothing in it says a given talk belongs on Thursday — add a room, reorder the list, or change the library, and it may land on Friday instead. Any one placement below is *an* answer, not *the* answer.

`needs/3` **describes** and lets the planner choose — any room seating 250 will do. `roster/3` **names** and accepts no substitutes — these four people. Both bind the same way once the search starts, which is what lets a room and a person clash for the same reason.

<!-- slide: code -->

```elixir
talks = [
  {"The Architecture Behind Deploying Livebook Apps", 40, ["Hugo Baraúna"]},
  {"From Complexity to Clarity", 40, ["Misael Perez Chamorro"]},
  {"Building Careers, Balancing Life", 40,
   ["Savannah Manning", "Anna Sherman", "Allison Randal", "Lorena Mireles"]},
  {"Extending Elixir with WebAssembly Components", 40, ["Chris Nelson"]},
  {"Starting a business on Phoenix LiveView", 40, ["Aaron Votre"]},
  {"Engineering Network Protocol Clients", 40, ["Carlos Souza"]},
  {"From Notebook to Production", 20, ["Emmanuel Pinault"]},
  {"Practical Mentorship for a Stronger Community", 20, ["Jordan Miller"]},
  {"Test-Driven Elixir", 20, ["Britton Broderick"]},
  {"You can build an AI agent", 20, ["Dennis Beatty"]},
  {"Mise en Place for Ecto", 20, ["Nicholas Henry"]},
  {"Leaving a BEAM cluster unattended for 2 years", 20, ["Yuri Oliveira"]},
  {"Elixir's Configuration Layers", 20, ["Stephanie Lane"]},
  {"Beyond Safe Migrations", 20, ["Joe Harrow"]},
  {"Introducing Elixir Observer", 20, ["Juan Azambuja"]},
  {"How LiveDebugger makes your life easier", 20, ["Krzysztof Nalepa"]},
  {"Telemetry-Driven Development", 20, ["Noah Betzen"]},
  {"Stranger Domains", 20, ["Nathan Hessler"]},
  {"Building Websites with Tableau", 20, ["Mitchell Hanberg"]},
  {"Writing Your Own Req Plugin", 20, ["Jacob Swanner"]},
  {"UXIDs in Elixir/Ecto", 20, ["Adam Kirk"]},
  {"Full-Stack AI with Elixir", 40, ["George Guimarães"]},
  {"Hey Ash, Let's Chat!", 40, ["Jinkyou Son"]},
  {"The Alchemy of Security", 40, ["Matt Ludwigs"]},
  {"Cat and Mouse: Adversarial Web Scraping", 40, ["Tyler Young"]},
  {"From Freakout to Fix", 40, ["Jonatan Männchen"]},
  {"Failing to Introduce Elixir", 40, ["John Darrington"]},
  {"Elixir is All You Need", 20, ["Christian Koch", "Jeremy Searls"]},
  {"Embracing the Monolith", 20, ["Igor Spiridonov"]},
  {"Building an Exception Tracker", 20, ["Gonzalo Rodriguez"]},
  {"Tips & Tricks for Building an App With LiveView", 40, ["Michael St Clair"]},
  {"Vibe Coding with Elixir", 40, ["Micah Woods"]},
  {"@auth and Beyond", 40, ["Ivy Markwell"]},
  {"Laying the Groundwork", 20, ["Kate Rezentes"]},
  {"Handoff - distributed DAGs for Nx", 20, ["Paulo Valente"]},
  {"AI Agents - Elixir is all you need", 20, ["Daniel Kentfield"]},
  {"How to be an Elixir champion", 20, ["Dan Janowski"]},
  {"IEx's Hidden Superpowers", 20, ["Brett Beatty"]},
  {"Are you mocking me?", 20, ["German Velasco"]},
  {"Phoenix and Ecto Taught Me Backend Engineering", 20, ["Doug VonMoser"]},
  {"Let's talk a bit about bits and bytes", 20, ["Geoffrey Lessel"]},
  {"From Bulbasaur to Venusaur", 20, ["Anna Sherman"]}
]

named = fn names -> Enum.flat_map(names, &List.wrap(Map.get(by_name, &1))) end

sessions =
  Enum.map(talks, fn {title, minutes, presenters} ->
    Agenda.session(title, lasting: Tempo.Duration.new!(minute: minutes))
    |> Agenda.Session.needs(:room, seats: at_least(250))
    |> Agenda.Session.roster(:speaker, named.(presenters))
  end)

length(sessions)
```

## Keynotes are pins

A keynote is published months ahead. It is not a decision left to the scheduler — it is a fixed point the scheduler has to work around.

A pin is not a special kind of value. It is an ordinary arrangement, so you build one by planning the session against the slot it was announced for and keeping the answer.

<!-- slide: code -->

```elixir
keynotes = [
  {"Keynote: Open Source Resilience", 60, ~o"2025-08-28T09:15/T10:15",
   ["Allison Randal"]},
  {"Elixir Update & Q&A", 40, ~o"2025-08-28T16:05/T16:45", []},
  {"Keynote: Elixir's AI Future", 60, ~o"2025-08-29T09:10/T10:10", []},
  {"Keynote: A New Case for Elixir", 60, ~o"2025-08-29T16:20/T17:20", []}
]

plenaries =
  Enum.map(keynotes, fn {title, minutes, slot, presenters} ->
    session =
      Agenda.session(title, lasting: Tempo.Duration.new!(minute: minutes), between: slot)
      |> Agenda.Session.roster(:room, [peninsula])

    case presenters do
      [] -> session
      names -> Agenda.Session.roster(session, :speaker, named.(names))
    end
  end)

pinned =
  Enum.map(plenaries, fn session ->
    {:ok, [arrangement | _rest]} = Agenda.plan(session, rooms ++ speakers)
    arrangement
  end)

length(pinned)
```

## Laying it out

Forty-two talks and four plenaries, against three rooms and forty-five speakers.

<!-- slide: code -->

```elixir
programme =
  Enum.reduce(
    sessions ++ plenaries,
    Agenda.programme("ElixirConf US 2025") |> Agenda.Programme.across(~o"2025-08-28/2025-08-30"),
    &Agenda.Programme.add_session(&2, &1)
  )

pool = rooms ++ speakers

{microseconds, {:ok, arrangements}} =
  :timer.tc(fn -> Agenda.arrange(programme, pool, pinned: pinned) end)

%{sessions: length(arrangements), milliseconds: div(microseconds, 1000)}
```

## Thursday morning

<!-- slide: code -->

```elixir
arrangements
|> Enum.filter(&Tempo.within?(&1.interval, ~o"2025-08-28"))
|> Enum.sort(Arrangement)
|> Enum.take(7)
|> Enum.map_join("\n", fn a ->
  "#{String.pad_trailing(Tempo.to_string(a.interval.from, format: :hm), 9)} #{String.pad_trailing(Arrangement.resource(a, :room).name, 14)} #{a.session}"
end)
|> IO.puts()
```

## The constraint held

There is no rule saying Allison Randal cannot be on a panel during her own keynote. It follows from her being a resource with a concurrency of one — and it would have followed identically for a room, a projector or a customer site.

<!-- slide: code -->

```elixir
for who <- ["Allison Randal", "Anna Sherman"] do
  arrangements
  |> Enum.filter(fn a -> Enum.any?(Arrangement.resources(a), &(&1.name == who)) end)
  |> Enum.sort(Arrangement)
  |> Enum.map_join("\n", fn a ->
    "  #{Tempo.to_string(a.interval.from)}  #{Arrangement.resource(a, :room).name}  #{a.session}"
  end)
  |> then(&IO.puts("#{who}\n#{&1}\n"))
end

:ok
```

## When it cannot hold

Force the panel into the keynote slot and the programme stops being satisfiable.

*Everyone free* is the operative phrase. Both other rooms are empty at 09:15 — the session still cannot be held, because one of the four people it names is on stage next door.

<!-- slide: code -->

```elixir
clash =
  Agenda.session("Building Careers, Balancing Life",
    lasting: ~o"PT40M",
    between: ~o"2025-08-28T09:15/T09:55"
  )
  |> Agenda.Session.needs(:room, seats: at_least(250))
  |> Agenda.Session.roster(
    :speaker,
    named.(["Savannah Manning", "Anna Sherman", "Allison Randal", "Lorena Mireles"])
  )

forced =
  (sessions ++ plenaries)
  |> Enum.reject(&(&1.name == "Building Careers, Balancing Life"))
  |> Kernel.++([clash])
  |> Enum.reduce(
    Agenda.programme("ElixirConf US 2025") |> Agenda.Programme.across(~o"2025-08-28/2025-08-30"),
    &Agenda.Programme.add_session(&2, &1)
  )

case Agenda.arrange(forced, pool, pinned: pinned) do
  {:error, reason} -> Agenda.explain(reason)
  {:ok, _arrangements} -> "unexpectedly satisfiable"
end
```

## Failures are sentences

This is the feature that saves the most time in practice, and it is a design decision rather than a nicety: eligibility and its explanation are computed *together*, so the reason can never drift out of step with the answer.

* Not `false`, not "no availability" — the resource, and what was wrong with it
* Every failing reason, not just the first one found
* `conflict/3` goes further: the *minimal* set of sessions in tension
* "Any two of these three fit — choose which one moves"

## Changeover

Agenda placed talks at 10:45, 11:25, 12:05. The real conference used 10:45, 11:35, 12:25. The ten minutes is changeover — one speaker unplugging while the next sets up — and Agenda does not invent it, because nothing asked for it.

<!-- slide: code -->

```elixir
{:ok, hall} =
  Agenda.resource("Hall", seats: 250, buffer_after: ~o"PT10M")
  |> Agenda.open(~o"2025-08-28T09:00/T13:00")

four_talks =
  Enum.reduce(1..4, Agenda.programme("Turnaround"), fn i, programme ->
    Agenda.Programme.add_session(
      programme,
      Agenda.session("T#{i}", lasting: ~o"PT40M", between: ~o"2025-08-28")
      |> Agenda.Session.needs(:room, seats: 250)
    )
  end)

{:ok, spaced} = Agenda.arrange(four_talks, [hall])

spaced
|> Enum.sort(Arrangement)
|> Enum.map_join("   ", fn a -> Tempo.to_string(a.interval.from, format: :hm) end)
```

## Model the gap, or carve it out

Turnaround is real time the room is unavailable, so it holds against a booking that already exists *and* against another talk in the same programme.

* `buffer_after` shifts the candidate starts too — 9:00, 9:50, 10:40, 11:30
* The published windows already spend those minutes: 40 + 10, 40 + 10, 20 fills 10:45–12:45 exactly
* Declaring it as well asks for the same ten minutes twice
* Model the changeover **or** carve it out of the hours — never both

## Scale

Shape matters more than size. 1,200 sessions across twenty days take about twice as long as 200 competing for a single day — six times the work for twice the time, because sessions that cannot interact are solved separately and concurrently.

| Programme | Time |
| --- | --- |
| ElixirConf: 46 sessions, 3 rooms, 2 days | ~175 ms |
| 240 sessions over 6 days | ~385 ms |
| 1,200 sessions over 20 days | ~2.5 s |
| 200 sessions competing for **one** day | ~1.2 s |

## Two days is not two problems

I assumed a two-day conference would split cleanly down the middle. It does not, and the reason is a person.

* Anna Sherman speaks on Thursday **and** Friday
* One shared speaker links both days into a single component
* One shared room, or one precedence, would do the same
* Worth knowing before assuming a long conference decomposes

## Where this stops

Agenda is a scheduler. Timetabling — a school or university week — is a different problem that shares the vocabulary.

* A timetable is a repeating *grid* of periods, not dated intervals
* A lesson splits: "five periods a week, at most two a day, one double"
* Feasibility is easy there; minimising student gaps is the whole job
* Every teacher and cohort recurs daily, so nothing decomposes
* That is a sibling library, not a flag on this one

